<!-- ACT 1: Person in a moment + Impossible framing -->
    <h2 id="refused">The Man Who Refused to Accept It</h2>

    <p>D.A. Henderson stepped off a plane in Delhi in 1967. He had a small budget, a skeleton crew, and a 10-year timeline. The local health minister told him the same thing every official told him: "Smallpox has been here for thousands of years. You will not end it."</p>

    <p>Henderson was an epidemiologist from the US Public Health Service. He had a calm voice, a stubborn streak, and no evidence that his plan would work. Every scientist who reviewed his proposal said the same thing. Too many villages. Too little vaccine. Too much poverty. The infrastructure does not exist.</p>

    <p>They were right to doubt. A disease that had killed 300 million people in the 20th century alone. A disease that could live in scabs, in blankets, in dust. A disease that killed 30% of everyone it infected. Humanity had never ended a plague. Ever.</p>

    <p>But Henderson had something the skeptics did not count on. He refused to believe a problem was impossible just because it had never been solved.</p>

    <!-- ACT 2: The obstacle + The low point -->
    <h2 id="melted">The Vaccine That Melted</h2>

    <p>Henderson's first problem was basic. The smallpox vaccine was a live virus that needed constant refrigeration. In remote villages with no electricity, no roads, and monsoon seasons that washed out everything, the vaccine arrived dead. Vials that cost weeks of transport time had to be thrown away. The workers found broken glass, spoiled doses, empty promises.</p>

    <p>In 2026, logistics is invisible. Trucks arrive. Packages appear. Cold chains are maintained. In 1967, getting a vaccine from a factory in Geneva to a village in rural India meant hours of heat, dust, and uncertainty. Half the doses were useless by the time they arrived.</p>

    <p>The second problem was the needle itself. The standard vaccine required multiple drops, multiple scratches, and a trained technician. You could not teach a villager to do it in a few minutes. You needed people who had studied for months. There were not enough of them. Not by a long shot.</p>

    <p>By 1974, the program was failing. Smallpox was still endemic in five countries. The budget was draining. Henderson called his wife. He told her the program might end. He was not sure he could finish what he started. She told him he had come too far to stop.</p>

    <!-- ACT 3: The breakthrough -->
    <h2 id="inventions">The Two Inventions That Changed Everything</h2>

    <p>The breakthrough was not a new vaccine. It was two things that look small but changed the math of eradication.</p>

    <p>The first: a needle. A young doctor named Ben Rubin invented the bifurcated needle, a forked piece of metal that held exactly one dose of vaccine between its prongs. A worker could be trained in five minutes. The needle used a fraction of the vaccine per dose. Suddenly, you could vaccinate a village with untrained workers carrying a box of needles in their pockets. No glass vials. No refrigeration. Just a needle and a pot of vaccine.</p>

    <p>The second: Henderson stopped trying to vaccinate everyone. Mass vaccination was the standard approach. It was failing because there was never enough vaccine for every person. So he flipped the strategy. Find every case of smallpox. Isolate the patient. Vaccinate everyone around them. Then vaccinate everyone around them. Let the disease burn through its fuel and die out.</p>

    <p>This was surveillance and containment. Ring vaccination. It did not require enough vaccine for every person on earth. It required enough vaccine for the people near every case. A much smaller number. The math finally worked.</p>

    <p>"I do not care if it seems impossible," Henderson reportedly told his team. "I care if it works. And this will work."</p>

    <p>Country by country, they went. They trained local workers. They paid rewards for reporting cases. They sent teams on foot, by jeep, by helicopter. They tracked the disease like detectives hunting a fugitive.</p>

    <!-- ACT 4: The result -->
    <h2 id="last-case">The Last Case</h2>

    <p>In 1975, Bangladesh: a 3-year-old girl named Rahima Banu was the last case in Asia. She survived. Workers vaccinated 18,000 people within a mile of her house in a week. No further cases.</p>

    <p>In 1977, Somalia: a cook named Ali Maow Maalin developed a rash. He was the last person on earth to catch smallpox naturally. He survived. Workers vaccinated 54,777 people in 12 days. No one else caught it.</p>

    <p>On May 8, 1980, the World Health Assembly made it official. Smallpox was gone. A disease that had killed pharaohs, emperors, and paupers for 3,000 years. Gone.</p>

    <blockquote>"Smallpox is the only infectious disease to be eradicated. This remains among the most notable and profound public health successes in history."<br>- World Health Organization</blockquote>

    <p>The last death from smallpox was not in Somalia. It was 1978, in a lab in Birmingham, England. A photographer named Janet Parker was infected by the virus grown for research. She died. All known stocks were moved to two secure labs. They still sit there today, debated every year.</p>

    <!-- ACT 5: You-relevance -->
    <h2 id="never-thought">The Thing You Have Never Thought About</h2>

    <p>Here is the part I cannot get over. You have never thought about smallpox. Not once. You have never checked your child for blisters. You have never wondered if that fever could be a death sentence. A disease that killed 300 million people in one century is simply not on your radar.</p>

    <p>That is not normal. That is the result of a man who refused to accept a 3,000-year problem as unsolvable. He did not have the internet, refrigeration in the field, or paved roads in some places. He had a forked needle, a smarter strategy, and a team that would not quit.</p>

    <p>And the result is that you live in a world where one of history's greatest killers does not exist. You do not know the name D.A. Henderson. Most people do not. That is fine. What matters is that someone, somewhere, looked at a problem everyone said was impossible and decided: "I will try anyway."</p>

    <p>Smallpox is gone. The way Henderson beat it, reframing the problem so the math worked, is the same pattern that works for any problem that seems too big to solve. Find the person who refuses to accept "impossible" as an answer. Give them a simpler tool. Let them work village by village. Then do it again until the problem runs out of places to hide.</p>

    <p>What are you telling yourself is too big to solve?</p>
